# BAZR -- repository ignore rules.
#
# Two kinds of entry live here and they are not equally forgiving. Build output
# that slips in is noise you can delete in the next commit. A key that slips in
# is public forever, because a public repository is cloned, mirrored and cached
# long before anyone notices. The secret rules come first for that reason, and
# each one says what it is defending against, so that nobody removes a line
# because they cannot see what it was for.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing material. Nothing in this section is recoverable once it is pushed.
#
# The upgrade authority for the deployed program is a keypair. Whoever holds it
# can replace the program binary at the published address, so a leak does not
# just cost the wallet's balance -- it hands over every account the program
# owns. The keypair is kept outside this repository and passed to each command
# explicitly (--keypair for solana, --provider.wallet for anchor, ANCHOR_WALLET
# for the scripts in anchor-program/scripts/). These patterns exist to catch a
# stray copy that gets made inside the tree anyway, which is how it usually
# happens: a file copied in for one command and then forgotten.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.keys/
.deploy/
id.json
*-keypair.json
*-deploy.json
*-deployer.json
*-wallet.json
*-deploy*.json
*-deployer*.json
*-wallet*.json

# idl/bazr_market.json is a committed artifact and is deliberately not matched
# by any pattern above: it carries no key material, and it is checked in so a
# client can be built without running `anchor build` first. Keep it that way --
# if a future pattern here starts matching it, .github/workflows/ci.yml fails,
# because .github/scripts/check-idl.mjs cannot read a file that is not there.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment files. These hold RPC URLs with provider keys in them, which are
# billable credentials even when they look like ordinary URLs. .env.example is
# the exception and is meant to be committed: it documents the variable names
# with empty or synthetic values, so a contributor knows what to set without
# anyone having to send them a real file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generator and packaging scripts. These are local tooling and are never part
# of a public checkout. They are listed by exact name rather than by a glob so
# that ordinary Python in the tree still gets committed -- for example
# tag-extension/scripts/make-icons.py, which is a real, documented build step.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
main.py
generator.py
builder.py
generate.py
grass.py
commit_history.py
_docgate.py
_docgate-exempt.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output and dependencies. Everything below is reproducible from source,
# and none of it should ever be reviewed in a diff.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
dist/
build/
out/
coverage/
.turbo/
*.tgz

# Rust and Anchor. `target/` is the cargo build directory; the compiled program
# lands there as target/deploy/bazr_market.so. Cargo.lock is NOT ignored: it is
# committed on purpose so that `cargo check --locked` in CI resolves the same
# dependency versions a contributor gets locally.
target/
**/*.rs.bk
.anchor/
test-ledger/
*.so

# Python bytecode. Running any script in the tree leaves this behind, and it
# has no business being counted, scanned or reviewed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor, OS and local tooling leftovers.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
.vercel/
.yarn/
.idea/
*.swp

# Packaged extension archive. `npm run build` writes it under build/, which is
# already ignored; this catches a copy made anywhere else.
*.zip
